
  [1mNext fork: how much the logger captures on its own.[0m The gap between "we have logging" and "we can actually debug
  production" is usually whether the unreported crashes get captured, not whether developers remembered to call
  [38;5;153mlog.info[39m.

  - [1mExplicit calls only[0m — a [38;5;153mlog.debug/info/warn/error[39m API, used where someone writes a call. Lowest volume, zero
    surprise, and you only ever see what a developer anticipated. An uncaught [38;5;153mTypeError[39m in the submit handler produces
    nothing.
  - [1mExplicit + global error handlers[0m — the above, plus [38;5;153mwindow.onerror[39m and [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m in the browser and
    [38;5;153muncaughtException[39m/[38;5;153munhandledRejection[39m in Node. This is what catches the failures nobody predicted, which is the
    actual production-debugging case. Small, well-bounded addition.
  - [1mExplicit + global handlers + auto-instrumentation[0m — also wraps [38;5;153mfetch[39m/XHR and keeps a breadcrumb trail of recent
    events leading up to an error. Best forensics, but it multiplies log volume, raises vendor cost, and re-opens the
    privacy question since request bodies pass through the wrapper — this login form would POST a password through
    exactly that path.

  Cheap to add later: auto-instrumentation and breadcrumbs, once you know what you're missing. Not cheap: discovering
  months of crashes were never recorded because nothing was listening.

  My recommendation is explicit plus global handlers. It closes the unknown-unknowns gap, which is the whole point of
  the request, and it stops short of the fetch wrapper that would put credentials back in the logger's path right after
  we designed them out.
